<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-linear-regression" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Linear Models</title>
  <p> 
    The methods of the previous few sections were descriptive
    statistics: ways to summarize and display information from a data
    set. Now I want to slowly move into inferential statistics. Now,
    instead of summarizing or displaying, I want to try to use the
    data in a data set to try to uncover some underlying truth about
    that data. I want to try to infer something. 
  </p>
  <p>
    There are many ways to do this, and we will devote the remainder
    of the course to just a few of the techniques of inferential
    statistics. We are starting with one important large category:
    regression. This is a category that applies to two numeric
    variables. A regression puts forward the idea that the two numeric
    variables are related by a mathematical function, where one is the
    independent variable and the other is the independent variable. In
    principle, any mathematical function can be chosen in a
    regression, and regression is commonly performed for many such
    functions: polynomials, trigonometric functions, exponentials
    functions, and so on. But by far the most common regression is
    linear regression.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before stating the definition that I want to work with in this
    course, let me remind you about the mathematical structure of a
    line. The most common equation of a line, where <m>x</m> and
    <m>y</m> are the variable, is called the
    <term>slope-intercept</term> form of the line. 
    <md>
      y = mx + b
    </md>
    In this setup, <m>x</m> is the independent variable, the input,
    and <m>y</m> is the dependent variatible, the output. <m>m</m> is
    the slope of the line, showing how steep the line is, with
    positive slope for upward moving lines and negative slope for
    downward moving lines. <m>b</m> is the <m>y</m>-intercept of the
    line: it shows where the line crosses the <m>y</m> axis. <xref
    ref=:figure-slope/> show a demonstration: this line has slope 
    <figure xml:id="slope">
      <caption>Slope-Intercept Form </caption>
      <image xml:id="figure82" width="80%">
        <asymptote>
          size(10cm,0);

          import graph;

          xlimits(-4,8);
          ylimits(-4,3);

          xaxis("$x$",Ticks("%"));
          yaxis("$y$",Ticks("%"));

          draw((-4,-4)--(8,2));

          draw((2,-1)--(6,-1)--(6,1));

          draw((-2,1)--(0,-2),Arrow);

          label("$y$-intercept $b$",(-2,1),N);
          label("Slope $m$", (4,-1),S);

          label("$y = mx + b$", (3,-3),E);
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>
    This slope-intercept form is the setup for lines that we will use.
    Now let me give the main definition of the section.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>linear regression</term> or <term>linear model</term>
        is a line chosen to match two numeric variables in a data set.
        The linear model is written in slope-intercept form.
        <md>
          \hat{y} = mx + b
        </md>
        The variable <m>x</m> is called the <term>explanatory
        variable</term>. The variable <m>y</m> is called the
        <term>dependent variable</term>. The notation <m>\hat{y}</m>
        indicates that the mdoel is an approximation of the data:
        individual data point are hopefully near the line, but the
        <m>y</m> values of the data points don't exactly match the
        <m>y</m> values of th eline. The coefficient <m>b</m> is the
        <term>intercept</term> and the coefficient <m>m</m> is the
        slope
      </p>
      <p>
        If the slope is positive, we say that the linear model
        demonstrates <term>positive correlation</term>. Likewise, if
        the slope is egative, there is <term>negative
        correlation</term> or <term>inverse correlation</term>.
        Positive correlations means an increase in the explanatory
        variable causes an increase in the dependent variable; inverse
        correlation is the opposite, where increasing the explanatory
        variable decreases the dependent variable. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    For the linear models we will analyze, the slope is by far the
    most important of the two coefficients. The slope indicates the
    strength of the effect of the explanatory variable. A high slope
    means that a small change in <m>x</m> produces a large change
    in<m>y</m>. Likewise, a small slope means that change in <m>x</m>
    have a pretty small effect. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Visually, we fit linear models to scatter plots. <xref
    ref="figure-scatterplot6"/> is a
    scatterplot of two numeric variables in a data set about 
    possums. The variables are the length of the whole animal
    (including the long tail)in centimeters, and the length of just
    the head, in milimeters. As usual, the dots are the cases,
    representing the measurements of one specific possum. 
  </p>
